  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nRadar imagery early this morning shows light rainfall traversing \nfrom west to east across the Upper Peninsula, with temperatures in \nthe mid 30s being seen ahead of the rainfall while temperatures rise \ninto the mid to upper 40s behind the rain (see the 1z obs for an \nexample). While the rain showers do look to fizzle out over the \neastern U.P. later this morning, a low pressure dropping down from \nnorthern Canada into northern Ontario today could start up lake \neffect rain showers back over the eastern U.P. as early as this \nevening and as late as Wednesday. While the CAA behind this low \ndoesn't look to be all that impressive, with at least one additional \nshot of CAA coming in Wednesday night/Thursday morning we can expect \nthe light lake effect rain showers to continue through at least \nThursday over the eastern U.P. In addition, there may be a rumble of \nthunder here and there within some of the showers as there is a \nlittle bit of CAPE in the boundary layer to work with. That being \nsaid, given that we will be under the warm sector of the \naforementioned low today (as already being evidenced over the far \nwest in the 1z obs) and can expect strong adiabatic lapse rates \nwithin the boundary layer on Wednesday, expect above normal \ntemperatures during the daytime hours today and Wednesday, with \nhighs getting into the mid 60s to even the lower 70s. In addition, \nthe low will bring gusty southerly winds of 25 to 35 mph to the area \ntoday, with a few gusts up to 40 mph possible along the shorelines \nof the Great Lakes in the east. Once the CAA starts, expect gusty \nnorthwest winds to impact the U.P. Wednesday into Thursday; expect \ngenerally wind gusts of 20 to 30 mph on both of those days, although \nthe winds on Thursday do look to be closer to the lower 20 mph. \n\nWhile we should see high temperatures return to around normal (i.e. \nthe 50s to lower 60s) for Thursday and Friday, expect well above \nnormal temperatures to return to the U.P. by this upcoming weekend \nas a Clipper lifts off the Rockies and splits over the western U.S. \nand Canadian Prairies. This will set us up under a Southern Plains \nairmass, allowing for high temperatures to reach up well into the \n70s across the area as lows only drop into the 50s Saturday night \nand 40s Sunday night.",
